  ///1HELP

  HELP {topic {subtopic ...}}

  HELP prints one topic of this file.  With no topic it lists the
  topics; with a topic it prints that topic and names the subtopics
  under it.  A topic name may be abbreviated to any prefix that is
  unambiguous to you -- HELP prints every topic the prefix matches.

  After answering, HELP prompts:

        HELP>

  Type another topic, or a bare RETURN to leave HELP.

  ///2File

  HELP reads HELP.HLP, on the current drive if it is there and on A:
  otherwise.  The file is plain text: a line beginning ///n names a
  topic at nesting level n, and everything up to the next such line is
  that topic's text.

  This HELP has no index and needs none: it scans the file.  DRI's
  HELP.HLP carries an index built by HELP [CREATE], and this one does
  not, so [CREATE], [LIST] and [EXTRACT] are not accepted here.

  ///1CCP

  The Console Command Processor prints the A> prompt and runs what you
  type.  A command is either built into the CCP or the name of a .68K
  program file, which the CCP loads into the TPA and calls.

  ///2Builtin

  DIR      list the files on a drive
  DIRSYS   list the files with the SYS attribute
  ERASE    erase files
  RENAME   rename a file
  TYPE     print a text file on the console
  USER     change the current user number

  ///2Submit

  When the CCP finds $$$.SUB on drive A: it takes its commands from
  that file instead of from the console, one line at a time, until the
  file is used up or you type a character at the console.  SUBMIT
  writes the file; see the SUBMIT topic.

  ///2Profile

  At cold start -- the first time the CCP runs after a boot, and only
  then -- the CCP looks for PROFILE.SUB on drive A: and, if it is there,
  runs it before its first prompt.  See the PROFILE topic.

  ///1PROFILE

  PROFILE.SUB is a SUBMIT file the CCP runs once, at cold start.

  Put in it whatever a session should always begin with:

        DATE
        SDIR [DATE]

  It is an ordinary .SUB file, so the SUBMIT conventions apply -- but
  nothing passes it parameters, so $1 through $9 expand to nothing.

  The CCP looks for it on drive A: in the current user number, which at
  cold start is 0, and only on a cold start; a warm boot does not run it
  again.  If it is not there the CCP goes straight to its first prompt
  and says nothing at all.

  ///1SUBMIT

  SUBMIT filename {parameters}

  SUBMIT reads filename.SUB, substitutes $1..$9 with the parameters you
  gave, and writes the result to $$$.SUB on drive A:, from which the CCP
  then reads its commands.

  ///2Parameters

  $1 through $9 are replaced by the parameters on the SUBMIT command
  line.  $$ is one dollar sign.  ^x is the control character x.  A
  parameter that was never typed expands to nothing.

  A line beginning < is console INPUT for the program the previous line
  started, not a command.

  ///2ReturnCodes

  A program's exit status reaches the CCP as BDOS function 108's program
  return code, and IF ERROR tests it, so a .SUB file can branch on a
  command that failed:

        MYCMD FOO.DAT
        IF ERROR
        TYPE OOPS.TXT
        ELSE
        TYPE FINE.TXT
        FI

  The CCP clears the code before every command it runs, so a program
  that sets none is read as having SUCCEEDED.  Each command this port
  builds sets it -- its C runtime publishes whatever main returned, or
  the status given to exit -- and so do the CCP's own failures, an
  unresolvable command name or a missing .SUB among them.

  A program you compile here with ZCC and link against the runtime that
  came with the compiler sets none, so IF ERROR reads success after it
  however it ended.  Test its work instead, with IF EXIST on an output
  file, or by reading it.

  ///1PAGING

  The console pauses at the foot of every page of output and waits for a
  key.  Any key continues; CTRL-C at the pause abandons the program, as
  it does anywhere else.

  Paging is OFF as this system is built, and it is a program that turns
  it on, through the System Control Block:

        offset 1Ch  @CONPAGE   lines to a page (0 means 24)
        offset 1Dh  @CONLINE   lines printed on this page so far
        offset 2Ch  page$mode  0 turns paging ON, any other value OFF
        offset 2Dh  pm$default what a warm boot resets page$mode to

  BDOS function 49 reads and writes them.  The pause is in the BDOS, so
  it applies to every program's output and not only to the CCP's -- one
  reason it is off unless asked for.

  ///2NotCtrlP

  CTRL-P is not a paging control here and is not one in CP/M 3 either.
  It toggles the printer echo: with it on, everything printed on the
  console is copied to the list device.  Type it again to stop.

  ///2CtrlS

  CTRL-S stops output and CTRL-Q restarts it, whatever page$mode says.
  That is the older and cruder pause and it is always available.

  ///1SDIR

  SDIR {d:}{filespec} {[options]}

  SDIR lists directory entries with their sizes, attributes and date
  stamps -- everything the plain DIR command does not show.

  ///2Options

  [SIZE]     show the file sizes
  [DATE]     show the create, access and update stamps
  [SYS]      include files with the SYS attribute
  [DIR]      exclude them
  [DRIVE=ALL] every logged-in drive
  [USER=ALL]  every user number

  ///1SHOW

  SHOW {d:} {[option]}

  SHOW reports on drives.

  ///2Options

  [SPACE]   free space, the default
  [DRIVE]   the drive's characteristics
  [USERS]   which user numbers hold files, and how many
  [DIR]     directory entries used and free
  [LABEL]   the directory label and its stamping modes

  ///1SET

  SET {d:}{filespec} {[options]}

  SET changes file attributes, directory labels and date-stamping
  modes.

  ///2Options

  [RO] [RW]        read-only, read-write
  [SYS] [DIR]      system, directory
  [ARCHIVE=ON|OFF] the archive attribute
  [NAME=label]     give the drive a directory label
  [CREATE=ON|OFF]  stamp file creation
  [UPDATE=ON|OFF]  stamp file update
  [ACCESS=ON|OFF]  stamp file access
  [PROTECT=ON|OFF] on a DRIVE: enforce passwords on it
  [PASSWORD=xxx]   the drive's own password, or a file's
  [PROTECT=READ|WRITE|DELETE]   on a FILE: what its password guards
  [DEFAULT=xxx]    the password programs open protected files with

  Nothing is protected until [PROTECT=ON] on the drive: a medium
  without that bit behaves as it always did.  A file needs a password
  before it can be given a protection mode, and an empty [PASSWORD=]
  or [PROTECT=OFF] takes both away again.

  ///1STAT

  STAT {d:}{filespec} {options}

  STAT is the CP/M 2 status utility, kept because it still reports what
  it always did: file sizes and attributes, drive space, and the
  logical-to-physical device assignments.

  SHOW and SDIR are the CP/M 3 utilities for the same ground.

  ///1PIP

  PIP destination=source{,source...}

  PIP copies files.  The destination may be a file, a drive, or a
  device; so may each source.

        A>PIP B:=A:*.TXT
        A>PIP NEW.TXT=ONE.TXT,TWO.TXT
        A>PIP LST:=REPORT.TXT

  ///1DATE

  DATE
  DATE SET

  DATE prints the host's date and time.  The host's clock is not set
  from here, so DATE SET only checks what is typed.

  ///1GENCOM

  GENCOM filename {rsxname ...} {[options]}

  GENCOM binds resident system extensions to a program file, so that
  loading the program attaches the modules first.

  ///2Options

  [NORSX]   strip the modules off again

  See also RSXLDR, which attaches a module by itself with no program
  attached to it.

  ///1RSXLDR

  RSXLDR filename.RSX {[RESIDENT]}

  RSXLDR attaches one resident system extension.  Without [RESIDENT]
  the module is temporary and goes away at the next warm boot -- that
  is, when the next program finishes.

  ///1GET

  GET [CONSOLE] [INPUT] [FROM] FILE filename [[ECHO]|[NO ECHO]]
  GET [CONSOLE] [INPUT] [FROM] CONSOLE

  GET makes a file the console keyboard.  Every character a program --
  or the CCP itself -- reads from the console comes out of the file
  instead, until the file runs out or GET CONSOLE puts the keyboard
  back.  So a file of commands, one per line, is a script.

  With [ECHO], the default, what is read is printed as though it had
  been typed.  [NO ECHO] keeps the console quiet; the commands still
  run.

  GET works by attaching GET.RSX, a resident system extension, so
  GET.RSX must be on the default drive.  A file that runs out removes
  the module at the next warm boot, and so does GET CONSOLE.

  ///2Limits

  [PROGRAM], [FILTERED], [NOT FILTERED] and [RAW] are refused by name.
  This GET is always [SYSTEM]: the CCP is a transient here and reads
  through the same extension chain as a program, so there is no way to
  feed one and not the other.  Nothing filters the stream either -- the
  file is served byte for byte, except that the line feed of a CR LF
  pair is dropped, since a console line ends at the CR.

  Only one GET at a time.  Modules cannot be relocated on this machine,
  so GET.RSX has one fixed address and it must be free.

  ///1PUT

  PUT [CONSOLE] [OUTPUT] [TO] FILE filename [[ECHO]|[NO ECHO]]
  PUT [CONSOLE] [OUTPUT] [TO] CONSOLE

  PUT copies console output into a file.  With [ECHO], the default, the
  screen shows it as well; with [NO ECHO] only the file gets it, and
  the screen stays blank until the capture ends.

  PUT CONSOLE ends the capture.  IT IS NOT OPTIONAL: it writes the last
  part-filled record and closes the file.  Output made after the last
  full record and never followed by PUT CONSOLE is lost.

  The file must not already exist.  PUT attaches PUT.RSX, which must be
  on the default drive.

  ///2Limits

  PRINTER, LIST and LST: are refused by name -- capturing the printer
  means intercepting a function this module does not claim.  So are
  [PROGRAM], [FILTERED] and [RAW], for GET's reasons.

  PUT and GET can both be resident, but PUT must be started FIRST: each
  module has one fixed address and a new one has to fit below the ones
  already there.

  ///1DEVICES

  This machine's logical devices:

  CON:   the console -- keyboard and screen
  AUX:   the auxiliary serial port
  LST:   the printer

  There is more than one console: STARTCPM -c n joins console n from
  another terminal.  A program talks to the console its process was
  started on.
